six selectors a modern application sends, and one of them decided whether it drew at all

iA Writer died on FIVE unrecognized selectors in a row, one per build, once Combine started
delivering values and took it into interface it had never reached. The last one left it with a
BLACK SCREEN: no window at all, and exit 0, which reads as a clean run.

  -[NSImage setCapInsets:]                    a resizable image
  -[NSControl setBaseWritingDirection:]       a cell property the control answers for
  -[NSTextField setBezelStyle:]               the same, to the text field cell
  -[NSTextField setLineBreakStrategy:]        macOS 11
  +[NSColor currentControlTint]               the accent, always blue here
  -[NSWindow animator]                        NSAnimatablePropertyContainer

WHAT IS REAL AND WHAT IS STORED, said here rather than left to be discovered.

  capInsets            stored. The nine part stretch is not implemented, so the image draws whole.
  baseWritingDirection FORWARDED to the cell, which already had it. Real.
  bezelStyle           FORWARDED to the cell, which already had it. Real.
  lineBreakStrategy    stored. There is no typesetter here with a strategy to follow.
  currentControlTint   NSBlueControlTint. One appearance, no preference to read, and blue is what a
                       Mac reports out of the box.
  animator             returns self, so a change through the proxy is applied AT ONCE. That is the
                       policy NSSplitViewItem already takes; a panel snaps rather than slides, and
                       the alternative is losing the change and the process with it.

The three stored ones are associated objects rather than ivars, because applications subclass
NSTextField and an ivar added here would shift the ones they declare. NSImage keeps a real ivar; it
is not subclassed by anything on the roster and the value is a struct.

CORRECTING MYSELF WITHIN THIS PATCH: I added preferredMaxLayoutWidth to the same category before
noticing NSTextField ALREADY declares it as an @property with an ivar that initWithCoder decodes
into. A category method would have won over the synthesised one and silently thrown that away. It
is not here. Grepping for the SETTER is what missed it: a property spells no method out.

MEASURED, iA Writer through scripts/app-drive.sh

